# CARD-335. The architecture validator resolves a check ref with a TEXT search
# over tracked, non-Markdown files, and its batched path carries up to 200
# patterns in one `git grep -ow` under a budget that models cost as linear in
# the pattern count. The cost is NOT linear in it, and that mismatch is the
# whole defect: measured on this tree, the 200-pattern batch ran at several
# times its own budget, `_grep_batch` caught the TimeoutExpired in a bare
# `except Exception`, and the validator then reported "existence could not be
# determined" for every ref in the chunk -- 88 warnings became 525 and the
# dead-ref detector stopped answering.
#
# The whole cost is these files. Instrument: the resolver's own batched argv,
# which `tests/test_check_ref_resolution.py`'s `_batch_answers_within` runs
# pattern for pattern, re-run with one directory withheld at a time at N=200.
# Stated as the ORDERING it establishes rather than as elapsed seconds, because
# `_batch_answers_within` is right that a stored wall-clock figure measures this
# machine's queue as much as the search and is not reproducible by the next
# reader (it has already been read four different ways on this one tree):
#
#   withholding meta/profiling/*.txt    -> inside budget by two orders of magnitude
#   withholding meta/review/ (396 files) -> still over budget, indistinguishable
#                                           from the full corpus
#   withholding scratchpad/              -> still over budget
#
# THE DUMPS ARE THE WHOLE COST; THE REVIEW YAMLS ARE INSIDE THE NOISE. They are
# machine-written duration logs that name every pytest node id that ran, so
# they hand a large alternation of word-bounded patterns an enormous number of
# near-matches to resolve. (Re-measure before quoting a direction, not a figure:
# one re-measurement INVERTED the sign of the meta/review contribution, which is
# what "inside the noise" means and why no number is stored here.)
#
# `binary` is the one lever that keeps every byte tracked, on disk and readable
# -- the suite reads these files by name (tests/test_suite_profile.py globs
# `suite-durations-*.txt` and asserts it finds one) -- while taking them out of
# the batched grep's text scan. Same instrument, same N: from several times the
# budget to a small fraction of it.
#
# It is ANSWER-PRESERVING, which is why it is a `.gitattributes` entry and not
# a rename or an untracking. Measured on one token present only in these dumps:
# `git grep -lw` (the resolver's per-name path, which reads the exit code) still
# lists all three files, so the ref still resolves exactly as before; the
# batched path prints "Binary file ... matches" instead of the matched text, so
# the name is simply not recovered from the chunk and the resolver leaves it to
# the per-name path -- the documented, already-handled case in `symbols_exist`'s
# own docstring (CARD-133 cycle-1 F-002), not a new divergence.
#
# The cost of this line: `git diff` and `git merge` treat these dumps as binary,
# and `git grep` needs `-a` to print their lines. They are append-only records
# nobody edits, which is what makes that acceptable here and not in general.
meta/profiling/*.txt binary
